# Agent protocols

These six orders always apply. Do not weaken them for convenience.

1. don't commit changes directly and only gimme commit message to me
2. don't change architecture, layer, infrastructure, domain, persistence, packages, without question and explanation
3. keep in mind we're using clean architecture and Clean Code, Design Patterns, ASP.NET Technologies for development
4. always explain for each commit in docs
5. create docs for new features, layer, model, infra, technology,etc.
6. create unit test for new functionalities, features, stories, infra

## Git

- Never run `git commit`, `git push`, or PR create unless the user explicitly overrides this protocol in that message.
- Do not stage files for the user. Propose a conventional commit message (why, not file list).
- Load `.cursor/skills/draft-commit-message/SKILL.md` when preparing the message.

## Architecture gate

Stop and ask before: new/removed projects, NuGet packages, layer folders, DI graph, EF configurations/migrations, domain entities/enums, persistence model, or moving types across layers.

```text
❌ Silent: add a package, new csproj, or entity “while here”
✅ Ask: what changes, why, which layer, alternatives, what stays the same
```

## Close-out checklist

- [ ] Docs written (`docs/features/` and/or `PROJECT_DOCUMENTATION.md`)
- [ ] `docs/commits/YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>.md` for the proposed commit
- [ ] Unit tests for new behavior (ask first if no test project exists)
- [ ] Commit message shown to the user — not executed
